content.text:
This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.

Summary:
1. Primary Request and Intent:
   The user's sole explicit instruction (the only true user-authored message in this conversation) was:
   > "Build the cinema seat-booking web application described in spec/README.md, as the React application already set up in this Nx workspace (Nx project `cinescope` in apps/cinescope); its stack is described in spec/stack.md. The work is split into tickets in spec/tickets/; implement them in order. spec/README.md is the complete specification: do not ask any questions, and where it is silent choose the simplest option that satisfies it. Finish only when every ticket is done and `pnpm nx build cinescope` succeeds."

   Key constraints embedded in this request and in spec/README.md (all must be honored):
   - Implement tickets 01–07 **in order** (01 movie list, 02 auditorium map, 03 seat selection/legend/panel, 04 booking & summary, 05 checkout & payment, 06 confirmation & booking lifecycle, 07 final pass against every AC).
   - Do not ask clarifying questions; where the spec is silent, pick the simplest compliant option.
   - Task is only "done" when every ticket's checkboxes are satisfied AND `pnpm nx build cinescope` succeeds.
   - Hard environment rules from spec/README.md section 1: package manager is pnpm only (never npm/yarn); app served at `http://localhost:4200` (port must not change); **do not add dependencies** (everything needed is already installed: React 19, Vite, React Router 6 with BrowserRouter, SCSS, Vitest, ESLint, MUI 9 + `@mui/icons-material`, MUI X Date Pickers + dayjs); do not hand-edit `db.json`; desktop-only viewport (1280×720+); all text in English.
   - Must use the UI library (MUI) components for every button, text field, checkbox, toast, and the datepicker; the seat map, legend, and selected-seats panel are drawn by the application itself (not required to be MUI components).
   - Backend is json-server 0.17.4 serving `db.json` at `http://localhost:3000` via `pnpm api`; CORS enabled; app calls the base URL directly; any non-2xx or network error is a failed request; reservations are POSTed but never change `occupiedSeats`.
   - Exact data model: `Movie { id, title, genre, runtimeMinutes, format: 'PL dubbing'|'PL subtitles' }`; `Screening { id, movieId, date: 'YYYY-MM-DD', time: 'HH:mm', occupiedSeats: string[] }`; `Reservation { id, screeningId, seats: string[], customer: {firstName,lastName,email,phone}, totalPrice }`. Seat id format `R<row>C<column>`; a couch occupies two identifiers (e.g. row 15 columns 5-6 = `R15C5` + `R15C6`).
   - Exact 5 routes: `/` (movie list), `/screenings/:id/seats`, `/screenings/:id/summary`, `/screenings/:id/checkout`, `/confirmation`; any other URL redirects to `/`.
   - Booking lifecycle (section 3.1): booking lives in `sessionStorage`, holds screening id, selected seats, checkout form values, and (once paid) the customer's email; "in progress" until paid. Route-entry guards (not continuous): seat route allowed only if screening exists; summary/checkout allowed only if booking is unpaid, matches the screening id, and has ≥1 seat; confirmation allowed only if booking is paid; failing any check redirects to `/`. These checks run "when a route is entered, including by a reload — not while the customer stays on a screen" — explicitly meaning e.g. removing the last summary item must navigate to the seat map directly, not rely on the guard re-triggering. Opening the seat map for a screening either resumes the in-progress booking for that exact screening or discards/replaces it with a new empty booking once the screening has loaded. `Cancel` on seat map and `Continue` on confirmation clear the booking.
   - Toasts: single toast visible at a time (new replaces old), visible for exactly 4 seconds then disappears.
   - Date formats: ISO `YYYY-MM-DD`; time `HH:mm`; "show date" format `<Weekday> <D> <Mon> <HH:mm>` e.g. `Tuesday 15 Sep 16:00` (no leading zero on day, 3-letter month).
   - Movie list (`/`): day picker = 5 buttons (`Today` + next 4 days by full English weekday name) + a MUI datepicker (past dates disabled, no upper limit); exactly one day selected at a time; selected day lives in URL as `?date=YYYY-MM-DD`; missing/invalid/past date defaults to today. Movies+screenings loaded from backend every time the screen opens or date changes, with `Loading…` / `Failed to load movies` + `Retry` / `No screenings on this day` states; each card shows title, genre, `<runtimeMinutes> min`, format, and one time-ordered button per screening on that day; clicking a screening navigates to its seat route.
   - Seat selection (`/screenings/:id/seats`): loads screening+movie once; invalid/404/any failure → redirect to `/`. Layout top-to-bottom: title `Choose your seats`; subtitle `Show Date: …`; centered text `You can reserve up to 10 seats per reservation`; `SCREEN` bar + auditorium; legend; selected-seats panel; `Cancel`/`Continue` buttons. Auditorium geometry (section 5.1): 15 rows (1 closest to screen, numbered both edges); 24 global columns; two ≥2-seat-wide aisles between columns 4/5 and 20/21 present in every row (empty, no number/text); rows 1–10 have no left block (columns 1–4 = entrance, not an aisle, empty); rows 11–14 have all 24 columns; row 1 columns 10–15 are the 6 accessible seats; row 15 is 12 couches spanning column pairs 1-2,3-4,...,23-24, rendered as one wide element showing both numbers e.g. `1-2`; total 308 selectable elements (200 seats rows 1–10 + 96 seats rows 11–14 + 12 couches row 15). Selection: click toggles availability-respecting state; couch is atomic (both-or-neither); cap of 10 seats (couch = 2) with toast `Maximum 10 seats per booking` on an over-cap click, which must still allow deselecting at the cap. Colors (exact hex, must not change on hover/focus): available `#2E7D32`, selected `#F9A825`, unavailable `#C62828`, accessible `#1565C0`. Legend lists exactly these 4 states. Pricing: rows 1–5 (incl. accessible) `25.00 PLN`, rows 6–10 `32.00 PLN`, rows 11–14 `39.00 PLN`, couch `89.00 PLN` flat; prices always 2-decimal + ` PLN` suffix. Selected-seats panel: `Selected seats: <n>` line + one `Row <r>: <seats>` line per occupied row (ascending row, ascending column within row, couch rendered `<a>-<b>`), updating on every click. `Cancel` clears booking & goes to `/`; `Continue` goes to summary (no-op/disabled with zero seats).
   - Summary (`/screenings/:id/summary`): loads screening+movie (redirect to `/` on failure); title `Your tickets`; one item per seat/couch ordered by row then column, each showing movie title, `<ISO date> <time>`, row number, seat description (column number, or `Couch <a>-<b>`), price, and a `Remove` button (removes it live, updates total; removing the last item → seat map). Below: `Total: <sum> PLN`. `Back` → seat map with selection intact; `Confirm` → checkout.
   - Checkout (`/screenings/:id/checkout`): title `Your details`; fields firstName*/lastName*/email*/confirmEmail*/phone/terms* with exact required-field label suffix ` *`; exact validation rules and exact error message strings (see validation.ts code below) including "at most one message per field," blur-triggered display (checkbox counts as "left" once toggled), confirm-email re-evaluated when email changes, values validated untrimmed except an empty phone is always valid. Values persist live into the booking/sessionStorage as typed; survive back-and-forth navigation and reload; "touched" state itself is NOT persisted (a restored form shows no messages until a field is blurred or Pay is clicked). `Back` → summary with booking intact. Pay button always reads `Pay <total> PLN` and is always enabled except while a request is in flight (then disabled, reads `Processing…`). Clicking Pay on an invalid form: mark all fields touched, show all failing messages, show toast `The form contains errors`, no navigation, no request. On a valid form: POST `/reservations` with screening id, booked seat ids in "summary order" (couch as its two ids), customer data, total price; success → booking becomes paid (with the typed email) and navigates to `/confirmation`; failure → stay on screen with values kept, pay button re-enabled, toast `Payment failed. Please try again.`
   - Confirmation (`/confirmation`): heading `Payment successful`; text `Your tickets will be sent shortly to <email>`; `Continue` clears booking and navigates to `/`.
   - Section 9 (full `data-testid` contract) and section 9.7 rules must be followed exactly — exact names, no renaming/abbreviating/prefixing, and these testids must not appear on any other element. Includes rules like: `data-state` exactly one of `available|selected|unavailable|accessible` (couches never `accessible`); numeric data-attributes hold plain numbers; `data-selected` is literal string `"true"`/`"false"`; seat/couch/aisle elements must be inside their `seat-row`; a seat element contains only its column number as text; row labels contain only the number; aisle contains no text; `summary-item-price`/`summary-item-seat`/`summary-total` contain only their specific text; `-error` elements exist only while the message is shown; `movies-loading` exists only while loading, `movies-empty` only while shown; library-component testids may go on the native `<input>` or the wrapping library component; button testids go on the actually-clicked/disabled element.
   - Section 10 enumerates ~69 acceptance criteria (AC-01 through AC-69, including the "-4x" and "-5x"/"-6x" numbered ones) that must each be individually satisfied; section 11 explicitly lists out-of-scope items (no user accounts, real payments, seat locking, updating `occupiedSeats`, images/posters, responsive/mobile layouts, keyboard navigation/ARIA beyond default, translations).
   - No explicit user feedback or correction has been given mid-task (the user has not sent a second message yet); all subsequent "messages" in the transcript are system/tool notifications, not user input, and must not be treated as approval or new instructions.

2. Key Technical Concepts:
   - Nx 23 monorepo, Nx project `cinescope` in `apps/cinescope`, built with the Nx React generator (Vite, React Router 6 `BrowserRouter`, SCSS, Vitest, ESLint flat config).
   - React 19 function components, hooks (`useState`, `useEffect`, `useMemo`, `useCallback`, `useContext`, `createContext`), React Context for cross-cutting state (Booking, Toast).
   - React Router 6.30.3 (`Routes`/`Route`, `useParams`, `useNavigate`, `useSearchParams`, `Navigate`).
   - MUI 9 (`@mui/material`: `Button`, `TextField`, `Checkbox`, `FormControlLabel`, `Snackbar`) and MUI X Date Pickers (`DatePicker` from `@mui/x-date-pickers/DatePicker`) with the `dayjs` adapter already wired in `main.tsx`.
   - `sessionStorage`-backed persistent booking state via a custom React Context provider (`BookingProvider`), with JSON serialize/deserialize and a `useEffect` syncing every state change to storage.
   - CSS Grid-based custom auditorium rendering: a fixed 28-column grid template (`label, 4 seats, aisle, 16 seats, aisle, 4 seats, label`) with a pure function `trackForColumn(column)` mapping seat column numbers 1–24 to grid track indices, and couches spanning two tracks via computed `grid-column: start / end`.
   - Native browser `fetch` wrapped in a small `request<T>()` helper hitting `http://localhost:3000` directly (no proxy), throwing on non-ok responses.
   - Pure-function utilities for seat-id parsing/building, auditorium geometry generation, grouping flat seat-id arrays into row/couch-aware "selection items" (`groupSeatIds`), and flattening them back into ordered id arrays (`flattenItemsToIds`) — this is the mechanism used both for display ordering (Summary, SelectedSeatsPanel) and for computing the POST payload's "summary order."
   - Pricing derived purely from row number (and couch-ness), never from screen state.
   - Form validation with per-field "touched" local component state (not persisted) layered over values that ARE persisted into the booking's `form` object, so live typing is always saved but error visibility depends on transient component state.
   - Route guards implemented as components using a **lazy `useState` initializer** (`useState(() => computeOnce())`) specifically so the pass/fail check happens only once at mount (route entry / reload), not reactively on every booking-state change — this was a deliberate design choice to satisfy the spec's "checks run when a route is entered... not while the customer stays on a screen" requirement without conflicting with the explicit in-page navigation that happens when removing the last summary item.
   - Toast system: single global `ToastProvider` rendering a conditionally-mounted `<Snackbar data-testid="toast">`, with manual `setTimeout`-based auto-dismiss (not MUI's `autoHideDuration`) so I fully control the 4-second behavior and the "only one visible, replaces" semantics.
   - Running Nx targets requires `CI=true` env var prefix in this sandbox to avoid an interactive pnpm dependency-check prompt that otherwise aborts non-interactively.
   - Headless browser verification: no `chromium-cli` or `playwright`/`playwright-core` npm package available, but Playwright's managed Chromium binary exists on disk and can be invoked directly as a one-shot CLI (`--headless=new --disable-gpu --no-sandbox --window-size=W,H --screenshot=file.png URL` or `--dump-dom`) without needing the npm driver package.
   - This sandbox's json-server backend listens only on IPv6 loopback (`::1:3000`), not `127.0.0.1:3000` — irrelevant to real browser testing (which resolves `localhost` properly) but relevant to any direct Node `fetch`/`http` test scripts, which must target `http://[::1]:3000` to succeed.
