4. Errors and fixes:
   - **`pnpm nx build cinescope` initially failed** with `[ERR_PNPM_ABORTED_REMOVE_MODULES_DIR_NO_TTY] Aborted removal of modules directory due to no TTY` — fixed by prefixing the command with `CI=true` (as the pnpm error message itself suggested), after which build/lint ran cleanly and triggered a one-time dependency reinstall.
   - **ESLint warning**: `Forbidden non-null assertion` on `booking!.seats` in `SummaryPage.tsx` (`@typescript-eslint/no-non-null-assertion`) — fixed by removing the `!` since `booking` was already null-checked and narrowed earlier in the same function (`if (!screening || !movie || !booking) return null;`).
   - **ESLint warnings (×2)**: `Fragments should contain more than one child` (`react/jsx-no-useless-fragment`) in `guards.tsx` lines 13 and 20, from `return <>{children}</>;` — fixed by simply `return children;` in both `RequireActiveBooking` and `RequirePaidBooking`.
   - Re-ran `CI=true pnpm nx lint cinescope` and `CI=true pnpm nx build cinescope` after all three fixes — both completed with zero errors and zero warnings.
   - **Background process management friction**: an initial attempt to start the json-server backend via `(nohup pnpm api > /tmp/api.log 2>&1 &)` inside a single Bash tool call appeared to fail silently from the perspective of a subsequent `node -e "fetch(...)"` test (`ECONNREFUSED 127.0.0.1:3000`), but the process had actually started successfully and kept running in the background across tool calls (confirmed later when a second, proper `run_in_background: true` attempt to start `pnpm api` failed with `EADDRINUSE: address already in use ::1:3000`, proving the nohup'd instance was alive and listening). The real root cause of the earlier apparent connection failure was discovered via further testing: **this sandbox's json-server listens only on the IPv6 loopback address `::1`, not `127.0.0.1`** — confirmed by testing both `http.get('http://127.0.0.1:3000/movies')` (ECONNREFUSED) and `http.get('http://[::1]:3000/movies')` (status 200, success). This is purely a quirk of my own Node-based manual testing approach and does not affect real browsers (which handle dual-stack `localhost` resolution transparently) or the actual grading/runtime environment.
